{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CESuuXW7C7UvJeLHchWBuN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose the next task.\n\n## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix utilities as the test bed. They derive formal specifications from C source code, generate Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verify parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline: extract → specify → synthesize → verify. Key components:\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Syscall abstractions (Darwin/Linux)\n\n\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n\n**Current State**: \n- All PRDs are written (prd001-prd010)\n- Use cases are written for all releases\n- Test suites are written for all releases\n- No code has been implemented yet\n- Current focus is on specifications, not code\n\n**Current Release**: rel01.0 (in_progress) - \"Batch 1 — ts specification and differential test\"\n- Use case: rel01.0-uc001-ts (in_progress)\n- This involves cmd/ts and pkg/testutils\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nI'm looking at what comes next in the pipeline.\n\nFor rel01.0, the dependency chain is clear: pkg/testutils needs to be built first since it's the differential testing harness, then cmd/ts can be implemented and tested against it. Since I'm constrained to one task and need to separate implementation from tests, the immediate priority is getting pkg/testutils built so everything downstream can depend on it. The most foundational piece is pkg/testutils - it's required by every utility's tests. Without it, nothing can be verified.\n\nLooking at prd001-testutils requirements:\n- R1: Test case data structure (DiffTest struct, Normalize function type)\n- R2: Binary execution (run two binaries, capture output, timeout, working directory)\n- R3: Comparison and reporting (normalize, compare stdout/stderr/exit code, report divergence, named subtests)\n- R4: Normalization hooks (per-DiffTest, TimestampNormalizer, composable normalizers)\n- R5: File-state comparison (for sponge, cp, mv)\n\nPer P2, I should split into types, implementation, and tests, but I can only create one task. So I need to pick the most impactful single task. The constraint says if types and interfaces are small enough, I can combine them with implementation in one task, but always split tests separately.\n\nSo the first task should be: implement pkg/testutils (types + implementation), targeting 250-350 lines. For requirements, I should focus on R1, R2, and R3 as the core functionality - the data structure, binary execution, and comparison logic. R4 and R5 could be deferred to follow-up tasks since they're more specialized features. I'm looking at the scope here—20 sub-items across five requirements is substantial, but it should fit within the 250-350 line budget if I focus on implementation only. The core pieces are the DiffTest struct, the main RunDiffTests function, binary execution with timeout handling, comparison logic, normalization utilities, and file-state comparison. Since the constraint limits me to one task and the guidelines say to split implementation from tests, I'll create just the implementation task covering all requirements. For file naming, the primary file should be `difftest.go` since DiffTest is the main exported type, and I'll need a separate `normalize.go` for the normalization functions. Looking at the acceptance criteria, I need to verify that the three files I've identified actually cover all the requirements—the DiffTest structure with its fields, the binary execution logic with timeout and working directory support, the comparison and reporting with normalization hooks, and the file-state comparison capability. The TimestampNormalizer and composable normalizers are key to AC3 and AC4, so those need to be in the normalize file. iffTest with file expectation correctly fails on divergence\n- AC5: Timeout test fails with clear message\n- AC6: golangci-lint clean\n\nLet me write the task now. I need to be careful about:\n- Self-contained description (stitch agent sees only this)\n- Anchored to PRD requirement IDs\n- File names derived from PRD/architecture\n- Design decisions from PRD, not invented\n\nLet me draft:\n\nTitle: \"Implement pkg/testutils DiffTest harness (prd001 R1-R5)\"\n\nFiles:\n- pkg/testutils/difftest.go (create) - DiffTest struct, RunDiffTests, execution, comparison\n- pkg/testutils/normalize.go (create) - Normalize type, TimestampNormalizer, ComposeNormalizers\n\nRequirements mapping:\n- R1: Implement DiffTest struct per prd001 R1.1-R1.4\n- R2: Implement binary execution per prd001 R2.1-R2.5\n- R3: Implement comparison and reporting per prd001 R3.1-R3.6\n- R4: Implement normalization hooks per prd001 R4.1-R4.3\n- R5: Implement file-state comparison per prd001 R5.1-R5.2\n\nThat's 5 requirements mapping to 5 PRD requirement groups - within the 5-8 range. For design decisions, I'm setting RunDiffTests to accept a testing.T, two binary paths, and a slice of DiffTest cases, and I'll use context timeouts with a 10-second default as specified in the PRD. I've got 7 acceptance criteria which fits the target range. Now I need to think about what information the stitch agent will need to execute this task effectively—the PRD details, file paths, Go module path, and project structure. I should include the `go.mod` file in the required reading so the agent has the module path, and I'm leaning toward splitting this into 2 files given the line budget constraints, with the main DiffTest struct and utilities in one file and the normalization functions potentially in another.\n\nActually, I think I can fit everything into a single file around 250-300 lines, but keeping normalization separate would be cleaner architecturally. Let me finalize the task structure now.\n\nOne constraint I need to respect: exactly 1 task. The pkg/testutils implementation is the critical foundation since everything else depends on it. I also need to make sure any design decisions I include come directly from the PRD or architecture—no invented choices.\n\nLooking at the PRD requirements, I can identify the concrete design decisions: the RunDiffTests function signature with specific parameters, the DiffTest struct fields, the Normalize function type as func([]byte) []byte, the 10-second default timeout, using t.Run for subtests, accepting a slice of normalizers applied sequentially, and supporting working directories with temp directory defaults. These are all explicit in the spec, so I'll document them as design decisions.\n\nNow I need to check the file naming convention from P7 — since the primary exported type is DiffTest, the file should be named difftest.go in the testutils package, not testutils.go.\n\nI'm organizing the code into two files: difftest.go will contain the main DiffTest struct, RunDiffTests function, and the helper functions for executing binaries and comparing outputs, while normalize.go will handle the normalization logic as a separate concern. Looking at the line counts, difftest.go should be manageable with the struct definitions, the main test runner loop, and the comparison helpers all in one place since they're tightly coupled through RunDiffTests. I'm verifying the line count is within the 250-350 range at around 255 lines, which gives me good headroom. The file-state comparison should stay in difftest.go since it's part of the DiffTest struct per the requirements. I should also ensure the stitch agent handles directory creation for pkg/testutils/ and that the acceptance criteria focus on compilation and linting rather than test passage, since tests are a separate task.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":73559,"cache_read_input_tokens":14003,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":73559},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2d7e90ea-5e59-4f1e-aed3-b59f62caecee","uuid":"cf985e3a-ddf9-4759-a9e4-81d2a66fd8eb"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CESuuXW7C7UvJeLHchWBuN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development -- deriving formal specs from C source code, generating Go implementations via AI, and verifying parity against GNU reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) with components: `cmd/` (per-utility Go binaries), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `pkg/sys/` (syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), and `magefiles/` (build targets).\n\n**Current state**: All PRDs (prd001-prd010), use cases, and test suites are written. No Go code has been implemented yet. The project is transitioning from specification to synthesis.\n\n**Current release**: rel01.0 (in_progress) -- \"Batch 1: ts specification and differential test\" with one use case: rel01.0-uc001-ts (in_progress).\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nrel01.0 requires cmd/ts with passing differential tests. The dependency chain is:\n1. **pkg/testutils** (prd001) -- every utility's differential tests depend on this harness\n2. **cmd/ts implementation** (prd004) -- the utility itself\n3. **cmd/ts tests** -- differential tests using pkg/testutils\n\npkg/testutils is the critical path. Nothing can be verified without it. ts does not depend on pkg/sys or pkg/format (per dependency-map.yaml), so pkg/testutils is the sole shared dependency for rel01.0.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement pkg/testutils differential testing harness (prd001 R1-R5)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml (full PRD for pkg/testutils)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/testutils component description and project structure)\n      - go.mod (module path and dependencies)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/testutils/difftest.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"DiffTest struct, FileExpectation struct, RunDiffTests function, binary execution, comparison and divergence reporting, file-state comparison\"\n      - path: pkg/testutils/normalize.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"Normalize function type, ComposeNormalizers combinator, TimestampNormalizer for strftime-pattern replacement\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement DiffTest struct with fields for Args ([]string), Stdin ([]byte, nil means no stdin), Env ([]string, nil means inherit), ExpectedExitCode (int), and Normalize (slice of normalize functions). Implement a Normalize function type as func([]byte) []byte. Per prd001 R1.1-R1.4.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement binary execution that accepts two binary paths (Go binary under test and GNU reference binary), invokes both with identical args, stdin, and environment, captures stdout, stderr, and exit code independently, imposes a configurable timeout (default 10 seconds), produces no output on passing runs, and supports an optional working directory (defaulting to a per-test t.TempDir()). Per prd001 R2.1-R2.5.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement comparison and reporting that applies the DiffTest normalize functions to both outputs before comparison, compares normalized stdout byte-for-byte, compares normalized stderr byte-for-byte, compares exit codes exactly, reports divergence with args, stdin (truncated to 256 bytes), reference stdout, Go stdout, reference stderr, Go stderr, and both exit codes, and runs each DiffTest as a named subtest via t.Run. Per prd001 R3.1-R3.6.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement normalization hooks: per-DiffTest normalize via the Normalize slice field, a built-in TimestampNormalizer that replaces common strftime timestamp patterns (e.g., 'Feb 19 12:34:56', 'HH:MM:SS', ISO-8601 datetime prefixes) with a fixed placeholder string, and a ComposeNormalizers function that chains a slice of normalize functions in order. Per prd001 R4.1-R4.3.\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Implement file-state comparison for utilities whose primary output is a file (sponge, cp, mv). DiffTest must allow specifying expected file paths and content after execution. File content is compared byte-for-byte with divergence reported in the same format as stdout divergence. Per prd001 R5.1-R5.2.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"RunDiffTests signature is RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary string, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) per prd001 AC1. This is the sole public entry point for cmd/ test files.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"DiffTest.Normalize is a []func([]byte) []byte slice applied in order per prd001 R4.3. When nil or empty, no normalization is applied. This replaces a single Normalize field with a composable slice.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Binary execution timeout uses context.WithTimeout with a default of 10 seconds per prd001 R2.3. The timeout applies independently to each binary invocation.\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Each DiffTest runs as t.Run(testName, func(t *testing.T) { ... }) per prd001 R3.6, where testName is a field on DiffTest so individual failures are identifiable in go test output.\"\n      - id: D5\n        text: \"When no working directory is specified, both binaries run in a per-test temporary directory created via t.TempDir() per prd001 R2.5.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"DiffTest struct exists in pkg/testutils/difftest.go with all fields specified by prd001 R1.1-R1.4: Args, Stdin, Env, Name, Normalize, WorkDir, and file expectation fields.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, tests) is callable from a cmd/ test file with a []DiffTest slice and no other setup, per prd001 AC1.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Both binaries are executed with identical args, stdin, and environment. Stdout, stderr, and exit code are captured independently per prd001 R2.1-R2.2.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"A binary that exceeds the 10-second timeout causes the test to fail with a clear timeout message rather than hanging, per prd001 AC5.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"TimestampNormalizer replaces strftime-formatted timestamps (e.g., 'Feb 19 12:34:56') with a fixed placeholder so that ts differential tests can pass despite wall-clock differences, per prd001 AC3.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"File-state comparison correctly detects when the Go binary writes different file content than the reference binary, per prd001 AC4.\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"All pkg/testutils code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint, per prd001 AC6. Run: golangci-lint run ./pkg/testutils/...\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":73559,"cache_read_input_tokens":14003,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":73559},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2d7e90ea-5e59-4f1e-aed3-b59f62caecee","uuid":"69183ab9-6d73-47f3-93f7-cc97fd0cac27"}
